     "Gigerenzer, long a director at the Max Planck Institute for Human Development in Berlin, has argued since the 1990s that the heuristics-and-biases school sets people up to fail with artificial puzzles. In the real world, he says, simple heuristics are often **ecologically rational**: fast and accurate in the environments they evolved for. Rewrite probability problems as natural frequencies ('10 out of 1,000 women') instead of percentages and most people, doctors included, get them right. His policy alternative to nudging is **boosting**: teach people skills such as reading risk, rather than steering them.",
