# 18 RECAP -----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTIONS.append({
 "id": "recap", "title": "Recap: what it changed, and what it did not",
 "intro_md": "**What it found.** People judge outcomes as gains and losses from a reference point, and losses weigh about twice as much. Defaults stick, irrelevant numbers anchor, plans assume the best case and the present beats the future. People pay to punish unfairness, and money can crowd out duty.\n\n**What it changed.** Pensions above all: auto-enrolment and escalation now cover tens of millions of savers. Tax letters, organ-donation laws, energy bills, a habit of testing policy with randomised trials, and consumer law that treats manipulative design as harm.\n\n**What it did not.** It did not prove people irrational. Many famous effects shrank or vanished, and fabricated data hit two star researchers. Nudges at scale are real but small.\n\n**India.** Udaipur and Tamil Nadu experiments, the 2019 Economic Survey, SEBI warnings, banned dark patterns and the taxman's NUDGE.\n\n**One line.** Behavioural economics did not overthrow economics. It made it describe humans.",
 "visual": None, "spotlights": [], "check": [], "source_ids": ["wiki_be", "pensionsage2025", "oecd_bi", "dellavigna_linos", "es2019"]})

