SIDE = {"area": "Myth and literature", "title": "Ulysses and the Sirens: the first commitment device",
 "body_md": "In Homer's Odyssey, the hero must sail past the Sirens, whose song lures sailors to wreck on their rocks. Ulysses wants to hear the song and survive. So he does something clever: he has his crew plug their ears with beeswax, then tie him to the mast, with orders to ignore anything he says and tie him tighter if he begs to be released. When the song comes, he does beg. They ignore him. Everyone lives.\n\nEconomists love this story because it is the cleanest picture of a **commitment device**: a choice made today, by your calm self, that removes options from your tempted future self. Thaler's cashew bowl is a small version. Save More Tomorrow is a pension version. So is a savings account you cannot touch until a date, a phone app that locks social media at night, or a bet with a friend that you will finish a project.\n\nThe interesting twist is that commitment only makes sense if you know you are weak. A perfectly rational person never needs to be tied to a mast. A naive person thinks they will resist and does not bother. The sophisticated person, who knows their future self will waver, pays to tie the knot. Behavioural economists call this being 'sophisticated' about present bias, and much of the work on saving, dieting and procrastination asks how to help people get there.\n\nTry it today: choose one temptation and tie one knot.",
 "image_ids": ["img_ulysses"], "card_ids": ["c51", "c52"], "source_ids": ["wiki_odysseus_sirens", "wiki_commitment"]}

